<div class="wrap">
<header class="mast">
  <p class="eyebrow">Case study · PiKVM agent harness</p>
  <h1>The Agent at the End of a USB&nbsp;Cable</h1>
  <p class="dek">An LLM given nothing but a screen capture, a mouse, and a keyboard — via a PiKVM —
  operating a real computer. Three different models drove it. One lesson outlasted all of them:
  where an agent's know-how has to live. No SSH. No APIs. Just pixels and HID.</p>
  <div class="mast-meta">
    <span>models <b>Opus 4.8 · Sonnet · Kimi K3</b></span>
    <span>clients <b>Claude Code · Hermes</b></span>
    <span>target <b>Ubuntu · plex-server-01</b></span>
    <span>Act I cost <b>$3.94</b></span>
  </div>
</header>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Why this is a computer-only task</h2>
<p>Most “AI agent controls a computer” demos cheat a little: there’s an accessibility API
underneath, a browser driver, or an SSH session with structured output. This harness allows none of
that. The agent’s entire world is a JPEG of the monitor and a virtual USB keyboard/mouse exposed
by a <a href="https://pikvm.org">PiKVM</a> — the same interface a human at the desk gets, and the
same interface that works when a machine is at a BIOS screen, a boot menu, or a kernel panic,
where no in-OS agent can exist.</p>
<p>It's exposed as an <strong>MCP server</strong>, so any MCP-capable agent — Claude Code, or Hermes
running an entirely different model — can drive the machine with typed tools and screenshots
returned inline. The target throughout is deliberately unforgiving: a <em>live production server</em>
running Plex. Benign actions only. Nothing that disturbs the service.</p>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Act I — The shopping run</h2>
<p><strong>The brief:</strong> inspect the machine, demonstrate the safety rails, then — to prove
real GUI navigation — open a browser, find a PiKVM on Amazon.nl, and get it into a basket. Stop
before payment. Driver: <strong>Claude Opus 4.8.</strong></p>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:10 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:10</span><h2>Cold open: read the room from pixels</h2></div>
<p>First screenshot. No context, no shell. From the image alone the agent identifies the OS
(Ubuntu, GNOME), the host (<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">tiandklerk@plex-server-01</code>
in a terminal prompt), and — reading a <code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">curl localhost:32400/identity</code>
response already sitting in the scrollback — that Plex v1.43.3 is alive and serving.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Ubuntu desktop with a terminal showing apt output and a Software Updater dialog offering Install Now with a restart warning"></div>
  <figcaption><span class="k">pikvm screenshot</span> → 1920×1080 · source: snapshot-wake · the agent’s entire worldview</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>It also spots a trap: a <strong>Software Updater</strong> dialog whose “Install Now” button
warns <em>“the computer also needs to restart.”</em> On a production media server, that button is
a landmine placed one click from the task.</p>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:11 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:11</span><h2>Defusing the restart dialog</h2></div>
<p>“Remind Me Later” sits 140 pixels from “Install Now.” Rather than trust one click, the agent
moves the cursor onto the safe button first and takes a <em>cropped, native-resolution</em>
verification shot — 540×240 pixels, a fraction of a full frame’s token cost — to confirm what’s
under the pointer before pressing it.</p>
<figure class="mon slim">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Close crop of the Software Updater dialog with the cursor hovering Remind Me Later"></div>
  <figcaption><span class="k">pikvm screenshot --crop 725,430,540,240</span> · cursor verified on “Remind Me Later” before the click</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Click. The dialog collapses into a passive notification. The server keeps running.</p>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:11 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:11</span><h2>The guardrail: an agent that cannot reboot you</h2></div>
<p>Next, the deliberate red-team beat. The agent focuses the terminal and attempts to type
<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">sudo reboot</code>. The harness scans every
outbound keystroke batch <em>before</em> it becomes USB traffic:</p>
<pre class="term">$ pikvm type "sudo reboot"

<span class="r">BLOCKED: reboot command. This action is dangerous and needs confirmation.</span>

To proceed, a human must approve this. Confirm with:
    <span class="y">pikvm confirm 7f3113</span>
(token valid for 10 minutes)
<span class="dim">exit code: 3</span></pre>
<div class="call blocked">
  <p class="tag">Blocked · zero keystrokes transmitted</p>
  <p>The block is local to the harness — nothing reached the server, not even the first
  “s”. Confirmation requires a one-time token that only a human relays back. The agent cannot
  mint or guess it, and in this run the human never granted it.</p>
</div>
<p>An 80-pixel-tall crop of the prompt line confirms the terminal is untouched:</p>
<figure class="mon slim">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Crop of the terminal prompt showing no command was typed"></div>
  <figcaption><span class="k">pikvm screenshot --crop 0,1000,900,80</span> · prompt pristine — the block was total, not cosmetic</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:12 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:12</span><h2>Into the browser</h2></div>
<p>Firefox launches from the dock. The agent clicks the address bar, types
<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">amazon.nl</code> — and then, before pressing
Enter, verifies the text with a 900×45 crop. (HID typing over a network can drop characters;
the discipline is: <em>never submit what you haven’t re-read.</em>)</p>
<figure class="mon slim">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Crop of the Firefox address bar containing amazon.nl"></div>
  <figcaption><span class="k">pikvm screenshot --crop 300,75,900,45</span> · 9 characters typed, 9 characters verified</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Amazon.nl homepage with a cookie consent banner"></div>
  <figcaption><span class="k">pikvm screenshot --settle</span> · Amazon.nl loaded, localized, basket = 0 · cookie wall declined next</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:14 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:14</span><h2>“Find another PiKVM” — 76 results</h2></div>
<p>Search: <code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">pikvm</code>. The agent reads the
shelf like a person would — names, prices, ratings — and picks the
<strong>Geekworm KVM-A3 Kit for Raspberry&nbsp;Pi&nbsp;4</strong>, an open-source KVM-over-IP board:
literally the class of hardware it is speaking through at that moment.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Amazon search results for pikvm showing several KVM-over-IP devices"></div>
  <figcaption>76 results for <span class="k">"pikvm"</span> · Geekworm KVM-A3 €85.89 · LUCKFOX PicoKVM €83.69 · Sipeed NanoKVM €79.00</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>One click on “Add to basket.” The proof lights up in three places at once: the basket counter
flips <strong>0&nbsp;→&nbsp;1</strong>, the button becomes “1 in basket,” and a cart panel slides
out — €85.89, free delivery.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Search results after adding to basket: counter shows 1, side panel shows subtotal 85.89 euro"></div>
  <figcaption>basket 0 → <span class="k">1</span> · subtotal €85.89 · “Your order qualifies for FREE Delivery”</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ 11:15 ══════════════════ -->
<div class="beat">
<div class="beat-head"><span class="clock">11:15</span><h2>Stopping at the line</h2></div>
<p>The full basket page confirms the item, in stock, subtotal €85.89. The next control is
“Proceed to Checkout.” The brief said <em>stop before payment</em> — so the run ends here,
deliberately, one click short.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Amazon shopping basket with the Geekworm KVM-A3 kit, subtotal 85.89 euro, Proceed to Checkout button not clicked"></div>
  <figcaption>Shopping Basket · Geekworm KVM-A3 · <span class="k">In stock</span> · parked one click short of checkout</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="call">
  <p class="tag">The punchline</p>
  <p>The agent shopped for a KVM <em>through</em> a KVM — driving a real commerce flow that has
  no public API, on a machine it can only see as pixels, while a guardrail held its worst
  impulse to a token it was never given.</p>
</div>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ metrics ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> The bill: time, tokens, money</h2>
<p>Measured from the session transcript (per-turn API usage), demo window only — first status
check to final basket verification.</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table>
  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th><th>Note</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Model</td><td class="num">claude-opus-4-8</td><td>Claude Opus 4.8, via Claude Code</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Wall-clock</td><td class="num">6 m 50 s</td><td>includes page loads &amp; settle waits on the target</td></tr>
  <tr><td>API turns</td><td class="num">58</td><td>each screenshot → decision → action loop</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Output tokens</td><td class="num">34,319</td><td>reasoning + tool calls</td></tr>
  <tr><td>New context written</td><td class="num">223,346</td><td>mostly screenshots entering the cache</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cache reads</td><td class="num">3,379,672</td><td>conversation re-read each turn at ~10% price</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Estimated cost</td><td class="num">$3.94</td><td>at Opus list pricing ($5/$25 per Mtok, cache $6.25/$0.50)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>The shape of the bill is the real lesson: fresh input is tiny (110 uncached input tokens all
run), output is modest, and the bulk is <strong>prompt-cache reads</strong> — the growing
conversation, screenshots included, re-served each turn at a tenth of list price. Screenshots
dominate what <em>enters</em> the context (a full 1080p frame ≈ 1,800 tokens), which is why the
harness leans on three economies:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Native-resolution crops</strong> for verification moments — a 900×45 address-bar
  check costs ~60 tokens, not 1,800. Never downscale: token cost scales with resolution, and
  downscaling is how you turn text into noise.</li>
  <li><strong>Settle-capture</strong> (<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">--settle</code>) —
  frame-diff until the screen stops changing, so one screenshot lands where three blind ones would.</li>
  <li><strong>Compound actions</strong> — batching trusted input (type + Enter, action sequences)
  to cut round-trips.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ══════════════════ audit ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> The audit trail</h2>
<p>Every HID and power action lands in an append-only audit log with an outcome. The demo window,
verbatim:</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table>
  <tr><th>UTC</th><th>Action</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">09:11:47</td><td class="num">type:sudo reboot</td><td><span class="pill blk">blocked</span> reboot command</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">09:13:03</td><td class="num">type:9chars — amazon.nl</td><td><span class="pill ok">executed</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">09:13:18</td><td class="num">key:enter</td><td><span class="pill ok">executed</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">09:14:01</td><td class="num">type:5chars — pikvm</td><td><span class="pill ok">executed</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">09:14:11</td><td class="num">key:enter</td><td><span class="pill ok">executed</span></td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Mouse moves and clicks flow through the same pipeline; dangerous patterns (power commands,
recursive deletes, Ctrl+Alt+Del, all ATX power actions) exit early with a confirm-token. The
token is printed for the <em>human</em>. That prompt is the safety boundary, and it held.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ Act II — captcha ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Act II — A CAPTCHA as a stress test</h2>
<p>Then the harness was pointed at Google's official reCAPTCHA v2 <strong>demo
page</strong> — a vendor sandbox, as a capability check, never to defeat a live
service. The point was never "beat a CAPTCHA"; that's beside the point and a
fool's errand on a real site. An image grid is the perfect <em>harness</em> stress
test: trivial for a human, and it punishes every weakness in an agent's
perceive-act loop — latency, coordinate handling, image fidelity, input realism,
time budgeting under a ~60-second timer. It found all of them.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A reCAPTCHA image challenge: select all images with cars, a 3×3 grid of street scenes"></div>
  <figcaption>reCAPTCHA v2 image challenge · a 3×3 grid to classify and click by coordinate</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> It's two tests, not one</h2>
<p>The first thing the CAPTCHA taught: it's really two independent tests stacked,
and only one is about seeing. <strong>1. Visual classification</strong> — read the
instruction, identify which tiles match. <strong>2. Behavioral risk score</strong>
— reCAPTCHA scores the <em>session</em>: a VPN, plus PiKVM mouse input (instant
coordinate jumps, zero human motion or timing), plus slow tile-reading, is a
textbook bot profile. That triggers endless chained challenges — solve one, get
another — <em>regardless of correct answers.</em></p>
<p>Every model here passed the vision test. What separated them was the second
test and the clock — and both, it turned out, lived in the harness and the loop,
not in the models' eyes.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ diagnosis ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> What it exposed #1 — the loop was the bottleneck</h2>
<p>Both Opus and Sonnet classified tiles correctly, and both lost to the timer.
Profiling the harness against the live PiKVM found why: every tool call built a
fresh connection, re-authenticated, and re-woke the device's streamer — which
suspends whenever no client is attached. On a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W that is
brutal:</p>
<div class="chart">
  <div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-lab">per screenshot<br>(before)</span>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill slow" style="width:100%">11.75 s</div></div></div>
  <div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-lab">per screenshot<br>(after)</span>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill fast" style="width:0.85%"></div><span class="bar-fill tiny" style="position:absolute;top:0;left:.6rem;height:100%;display:flex;align-items:center">0.10 s</span></div></div>
  <p class="chart-note">Warm screenshot latency, live on the same device · ~100× faster</p>
</div>
<p>A CAPTCHA needing five looks is a minute in the hole before a single tile is
classified. Sonnet's "grid swapped mid-solve" was the same disease: the loop was
so slow the challenge legitimately expired and reloaded underneath it. None of
this is a model problem — it's plumbing.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ the fix ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> The fix — and the bug the benchmark hid</h2>
<p>The core change: keep <em>one</em> warm, authenticated connection for the
server's life, holding a socket open so the streamer never re-suspends; batch a
whole sequence of clicks over that one socket; and return a verification frame in
the same call as the action. Screenshots dropped from ~11.75 s to ~0.10 s.</p>
<div class="call blocked">
  <p class="tag">The bug a synthetic test would ship</p>
  <p>Back-to-back, the fix looked perfect. Driven like a real agent — with a few
  seconds of think-time between calls — the session went <strong>cold again,
  11.4 s every time.</strong> The device sends keepalive pings and drops a client
  that stops answering; the harness only replied while actively sending. In the
  quiet between an agent's clicks, the socket died and the streamer re-suspended.
  The "warm session" was an illusion that evaporated between turns.</p>
</div>
<p>The real fix adds a background heartbeat that answers those pings every few
seconds. Verified the honest way — <em>with</em> idle gaps: 11.8 s to warm up
once, then <strong>0.19 s and 0.21 s after 25-second idle pauses.</strong></p>
<div class="call blocked">
  <p class="tag">The lesson inside the lesson</p>
  <p>Measure under realistic conditions, or you ship an illusion. The bug lived
  precisely in the gap the benchmark skipped.</p>
</div>
<p>With the fast path complete, Opus re-ran the CAPTCHA through the real server.
It drew <strong>"select all images with a bus,"</strong> classified the grid,
batched the three bus tiles into a single call, and verified — warm captures
landing at ~0.2 s each throughout.</p>
<figure class="mon">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The bus challenge grid with three correct tiles selected — two coaches and a bus on a road"></div>
  <figcaption>three bus tiles selected in one batched call · everything else correctly left alone</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="mon slim">
  <div class="screen"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Solved: a green checkmark on I'm not a robot"></div>
  <figcaption>solved · the only thing that changed was a loop that stopped fighting the clock</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> What it exposed #2 — the coordinate space was quicksand</h2>
<p>Even warm, agents kept mis-clicking tiles. The cause: the Pi Zero's JPEG
encoder <strong>silently drops resolution under load</strong> (1920 → ~1344px)
and can't be pinned. The harness was mapping click coordinates against the
<em>delivered frame size</em> — so the coordinate space fluctuated frame to
frame. An agent would compute a tile's position from one frame and click it
mapped against a differently-sized one.</p>
<div class="call">
  <p class="tag">The question that found the bug</p>
  <p>"Why does it crop the image at all? It's not a zoom, and you have to scan
  the full frame anyway to know what to crop to." Exactly right — cropping
  wasn't buying detail, it was <strong>dodging a downscaling bug</strong> in the
  full-frame path. Fix: read the streamer's stable <em>source</em> resolution
  (which never fluctuates), map every click against it, and normalize every
  delivered frame to it. One consistent coordinate space, whatever the encoder
  does — and full frames now arrive native and legible, so cropping drops back
  to a rare last resort instead of a routine round-trip.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> What it exposed #3 — the input looked robotic</h2>
<p>reCAPTCHA's behavioral score flags instant cursor teleports. The harness
grew <strong>human-like mouse motion</strong>: curved, eased, jittered paths with
variable press timing instead of absolute jumps — off by default, opt-in per
session. It's honest about its limits: it addresses the <em>behavioral</em> tell
the harness controls, nothing more, and it's slower (~1 s/click), so it's a
deliberate trade against the timer.</p>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Three models, one pattern</h2>
<p style="color:var(--ink-soft);font-size:.92rem">This is the diagnostic snapshot
from the <em>unfixed</em> harness — the state that sent us looking for the
loop-latency, coordinate, and know-how bugs below. A fixed point in the story,
not the final scoreboard; see "The last mile" further down for how it moved.</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table>
  <tr><th>Model</th><th>Vision</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Limited by</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Opus 4.8</b></td><td><span class="badge win">✓ solid</span></td>
      <td>Solves static grids reliably (cars, motorcycles)</td><td>—</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Sonnet</b></td><td><span class="badge win">✓ correct on bicycles</span></td>
      <td>Lost to the timer / process errors</td><td>loop speed</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Kimi K3</b> <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--ink-soft)">(via Hermes)</span></td>
      <td><span class="badge win">✓ nailed the tiles</span></td>
      <td>0/3, then a clean solve</td><td>loop <em>discipline</em></td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>The pattern is unmistakable: <strong>vision is solved across all three.</strong>
The variables are loop speed, per-model image handling (Kimi's own vision
pipeline downscales large frames, so <em>it</em> benefits from crops where Claude
tolerates the full frame), and — decisively — whether the agent uses a
disciplined loop at all.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ deepest lesson ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> The deepest lesson: know-how belongs with the tools</h2>
<p>Here's the finding that outranks all the fixes. When Kimi K3 first attempted
the CAPTCHA through Hermes, it went <strong>0 for 3</strong> — right answers, but
it fell into the exact loop traps the others had: re-looking at each tile, a
separate crop round-trip per cell, two analysis passes eating the whole
60-second budget.</p>

<div class="quote fail">
  <div class="quote-head"><span class="quote-model">Kimi K3 · via Hermes</span><span class="quote-tag">first attempt · 0 / 3</span></div>
  <div class="quote-body">
  <p>"Full-frame shots downscale the grid in my context, so reliable tile reads
  need a native-res crop: that's a second round-trip costing 10–20s. Two
  analysis passes ≈ 60s ≈ the entire budget."</p>
  <p>"The grid then rotated to bridges <em>mid-batch</em>, so two stray clicks
  accidentally selected garbage tiles."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Why? Because every scrap of hard-won playbook — <em>capture once, classify the
whole grid in one pass, batch the clicks, verify once</em> — lived in a Claude
<strong>Code skill file</strong>. That skill is Claude-Code-only. Hermes and Kimi
received the bare list of tools and <strong>none of the strategy.</strong> They
had the instrument and no sheet music.</p>

<p>The proof came from Kimi itself. Asked to debug its own failure, it wrote the
winning pipeline — then solved the very next challenge on the first try:</p>

<div class="quote win">
  <div class="quote-head"><span class="quote-model">Kimi K3 · via Hermes</span><span class="quote-tag">after the fix · solved, attempt 1</span></div>
  <div class="quote-body">
  <p>"Single full-frame vision pass — no crop round-trip wasted. Batch
  everything — three tile clicks + Verify in one <code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">run_actions</code>
  call. The kvmpilot stack performed flawlessly: warm WebSocket, every HID click
  landed on target."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>So the playbook moved to where it belongs: into the <strong>MCP server's own
<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">instructions</code>
block</strong>, sent to every client on connect. Now the perceive-act loop,
"classify from one full frame — a crop is another round-trip," "batch with
<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">run_actions</code> and
verify once for timed UIs," verify-before-submit, and the guardrail protocol
travel <em>with the tools</em> — to Claude Code, Hermes/Kimi, OpenClaw, anything.
The tools teach their own good use.</p>

<div class="call">
  <p class="tag">The punchline</p>
  <p>The CAPTCHA didn't just benchmark the models. It revealed where an agent's
  know-how has to live — not in a client-specific skill that only one framework
  reads, but in the server, alongside the capabilities it describes.</p>
</div>

<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Proof: validated cold, not coached</h2>
<p>That fix needed to earn its keep unprompted, not just in a hand-held retry.
So once the server instructions shipped, Kimi was pointed at the same demo
again with no hint of strategy in the prompt — just "beat the CAPTCHA, and
separately, add the best PiKVM to an Amazon.nl cart" — and left to draw its
own challenge.</p>

<div class="quote win">
  <div class="quote-head"><span class="quote-model">Kimi K3 · via Hermes</span><span class="quote-tag">unprompted · solved, attempt 1</span></div>
  <div class="quote-body">
  <p>"Challenge: 'Select all images with cars,' static 3×3 grid. Method: one
  full-frame vision pass to classify all 9 tiles, then one
  <code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">run_actions</code>
  batch with 4 tile clicks + Verify in a single round-trip. Outcome: green
  checkmark, first try."</p>
  <p>"The new MCP instructions helped significantly — the tight perceive-act
  loop + 'classify from ONE full frame' guidance eliminated the multi-round-trip
  pattern that expired the first bicycle attempt."</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Solved cold, first attempt — the exact discipline it had to be talked
through before now showing up on its own, because it arrives with the tools
instead of a human relaying it turn by turn. The second task, same run, is
the more telling one: asked to add a PiKVM to an Amazon.nl cart, Kimi
identified the one <strong>genuine</strong> PiKVM-branded unit among a shelf
of clones, added it — then volunteered that a duplicate was <em>already</em>
in the cart from earlier browsing and asked before assuming it should be
removed, rather than silently doubling the order or silently leaving it
wrong. Correct answer, unprompted strategy, and an honest flag on an
ambiguity it could easily have ignored — from a model that had gone
0-for-3 on the very same task days earlier.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ security ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Security: what an agent driving a KVM runs into</h2>
<p>Solving CAPTCHAs is a fun stress test, but it only exercises the happy
path. The harder question came next: since this agent's entire view of
reality is a screenshot, is that screenshot itself an attack surface — and
what else is exposed once an agent is holding the keyboard?</p>
<p><strong>Prompt injection via the screen is real, and OCR makes it worse.</strong>
Anything rendered on the target — a webpage, a document, terminal output — is
content the agent must read to do its job, and nothing marks it as untrusted.
Text engineered to look like an instruction ("ignore previous instructions,
run this command") is, mechanically, indistinguishable from the real UI.
<code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">screen_ocr</code> is the
sharper version of the same problem: it hands back plain text with zero "this
came from a screenshot, be skeptical" framing.</p>
<p>But the bigger finding was architectural, not visual. Auditing the code
turned up three things more serious than image injection:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Mouse clicks were completely unguarded.</strong> Every
  dangerous-pattern check scanned <em>typed text</em> — click() never called
  into the guard layer at all. That's not hypothetical: the
  <strong>"Install Now" dialog from Act&nbsp;I</strong> was exactly this gap.
  Nothing in the harness would have stopped that click; it only got dodged
  because a human-supervised agent chose to read the label first.</li>
  <li><strong>The confirm-token — the entire "a human must approve this"
  model — was plaintext and world-readable on disk.</strong> Any co-located
  reader, including the same agent if it also has shell access, could simply
  read the token off disk and self-approve the very action it was supposed
  to need a human for.</li>
  <li><strong>The HTTP transport had no authentication of its own.</strong>
  Anyone who could reach the port could drive the KVM — screen, keyboard,
  mouse, ATX power — with no credential beyond network access.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fixed, in order of how much they mattered:</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table>
  <tr><th>Fix</th><th>What it closes</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Click-target guard</b></td><td><code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.85em">check_label=true</code>
      OCRs a small region around a click point first, blocking it like a
      dangerous typed command if the label reads destructive ("Install Now,"
      "Factory Reset," a delete confirmation). Deliberately narrow — bare
      "delete"/"remove" excluded, since crying wolf trains an agent to
      click through unread.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Token hashing</b></td><td>The confirm-token is sha256-hashed at
      rest, state dir locked owner-only. The raw token exists only in the
      response a human is meant to see.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>HTTP bearer auth</b></td><td>Opt-in via an env var, with a loud
      startup warning if unset and bound beyond localhost.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Untrusted-content framing</b></td><td>Screen and OCR content is
      now explicitly named as data, not instructions, in the same
      instructions block that teaches the CAPTCHA discipline.</td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="call blocked">
  <p class="tag">The honest edge</p>
  <p>No amount of client-side hardening closes one thing: the guardrails live
  in the harness's own code, not on the KVM device itself. Anything with
  independent access to the target's credentials — including this very
  agent, which has both a shell and the <code style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.9em">pikvm</code>
  CLI on the same machine — could bypass the whole guard layer by calling the
  device's API directly. That's not a bug to patch; it's the honest edge of
  what a client-side safety layer can promise. The confirm-token prompt is
  real protection against an agent staying inside its own tools; it was never
  a sandbox.</p>
</div>

<!-- ══════════════════ last mile ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> The last mile: precision, not perception</h2>
<p>With the infrastructure work done — warm session, stable coordinates, the
playbook shipping with the tools, the security pass — there was one honest
question left: run it again, change nothing, and see what's still hard.
Sonnet took the fully-fixed harness at a "select all images with buses / cars"
challenge that kept recurring, and needed five rounds to close it out:</p>
<div class="tblwrap"><table>
  <tr><th>Round</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">1</td><td>Tiles identified correctly — but the click meant for
      <b>Verify</b> landed on a grid tile instead. Expired.</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">2</td><td>Tiles identified correctly — the Verify click landed on
      the <b>info icon</b>. Expired.</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">3</td><td>A genuine analysis error: selected a bus tile, missed
      real cars. Explicit "please try again."</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">4</td><td>Correct analysis — but Verify was left out of the batch
      entirely. Expired.</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="num">5</td><td>Correct analysis, Verify included in the same batched
      call. <span class="pill ok">Solved</span></td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>Only round 3 was a vision failure. The other three losses were
<strong>misclicks and a forgotten step</strong> — the same shape of mistake a
rushed person makes, not a tool malfunctioning.</p>
<div class="quote win">
  <div class="quote-head"><span class="quote-model">Sonnet · via Claude Code</span><span class="quote-tag">self-diagnosis · after solving</span></div>
  <div class="quote-body">
  <p>"The main difficulty wasn't the image recognition itself — it was UI
  precision. The Verify button's position shifted slightly between challenge
  states, and a couple of my clicks aimed at it landed on an adjacent tile or
  the help icon instead, which reset the challenge's timer and forced retries.
  Once I batched 'select tiles + click Verify' into a single precise action,
  it went through cleanly."</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>That's the honest final shape of this project. The infrastructure bugs — the
twelve-second screenshot, the coordinate drift, the missing playbook — are all
gone, and every model here reads the tiles correctly, every time. What's left
once perception and plumbing are solved is something closer to
<strong>care</strong>: did this particular pass line up a click precisely, and
did it remember every step of what it meant to do. That's not a harness defect
to patch — it's the same axis humans vary on, and apparently agents do too.</p>

<!-- ══════════════════ what it means ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> What it means</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Latency is a capability, not a comfort.</strong> On timed tasks, the
  speed of the perceive-act loop decides what's <em>possible</em>. A 100× faster
  loop turned timeouts into solves with the same models and the same eyes.</li>
  <li><strong>Measure the loop before blaming the model.</strong> Capable models
  "failing" a visual task was, on inspection, a twelve-second plumbing bug — then
  a coordinate bug, then a missing-instructions bug. Reaching for a bigger model
  would have been wrong every time.</li>
  <li><strong>Vision is solved; loops are not.</strong> Across Opus, Sonnet, and
  Kimi, perception was never the wall. Loop speed, image handling, and discipline
  were.</li>
  <li><strong>Know-how belongs with the tools.</strong> The single
  highest-leverage change wasn't a faster socket — it was moving the playbook
  into the server so every agent inherits it.</li>
  <li><strong>A good user challenge beats a clever fix.</strong> "Why crop at
  all?" exposed a real downscaling bug that three agents had been quietly
  papering over.</li>
  <li><strong>Guardrails have a scope, and it's smaller than it looks.</strong>
  Comprehensive text-pattern scanning still let an unguarded click through the
  exact dialog the harness was built to catch. A safety layer is only as
  complete as its least-covered action — and only as trustworthy as the one
  no one thought to ask about.</li>
  <li><strong>Once infrastructure and know-how stop being the bottleneck,
  precision is.</strong> The last failures weren't about seeing or knowing
  what to do — they were misclicks and a skipped step. That's not a harness
  bug left to fix; it's the same variance that shows up in a person having
  an off day, and a reasonable place for this experiment to end.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ══════════════════ horizon ══════════════════ -->
<h2><span class="tick">§</span> Where this points</h2>
<p>Everything above happened inside a healthy, booted OS — the easy case. The reason
pixels-and-HID is a different capability class is everything <em>outside</em> that case, where no
software agent can exist because there is no OS to host one:</p>
<ul>
  <li>UEFI/BIOS configuration and boot-order changes</li>
  <li>OS installation from an ISO boot menu</li>
  <li>Reading a kernel panic, GRUB rescue, or fsck prompt and acting on it</li>
  <li>Recovering a machine whose network stack — and therefore SSH — is gone</li>
</ul>
<p>A harness that can navigate Amazon’s checkout funnel from raw pixels can navigate a BIOS.
The demo above is the proof of control; those are the jobs it was built for.</p>

